# [[.Name]]

This development template requires matching unreleased core and contrib APIs,
including the shared transport/client, selector/random[[if .HTTP]] and the contrib HTTP backend[[end]]. Use reviewed matching source revisions
for local validation and refresh dependency versions/checksums before a public-proxy build.

An application-owned, single-module Go project generated by nostosx-cli. Normal builds and startup do not require the CLI.
Enabled transports: **[[.Transport]]**. Added services inherit these transports without enabling another protocol.

[简体中文](README.zh-CN.md)

## Start

Use Go 1.27.3, Task v3.53.1 and GOWORK=off:

```sh
export GOWORK=off
go mod download
[[if .NeedsToken]]export API_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"[[end]]
[[if eq .Layout "monolith"]]go run ./cmd/server[[end]]
```

[[if eq .Layout "microservices"]]Add a context and service before running its standalone entry. No service process exists initially.[[else]]The initial host exposes no business routes. Ctrl+C shuts it down gracefully.[[end]]
[[if .HTTP]]HTTP defaults to `127.0.0.1:8080`; use `-addr` to change it. `/livez` and `/readyz` are public health endpoints.[[end]]
[[if .GRPC]]gRPC defaults to `127.0.0.1:9090`; use `-grpc-addr` to change it. Every RPC, including health probes, requires `authorization: Bearer <API_TOKEN>`.[[end]]
[[if .NeedsToken]]API_TOKEN requires at least 32 printable non-whitespace ASCII characters. Never log or commit it.[[end]]
Plaintext listeners accept only literal loopback IPs. Define TLS, identity and per-operation authorization before external deployment; see SECURITY.md.
Commands use a POSIX shell; Windows users can use Git Bash.

## Contexts and services

```sh
export NOSTOSX=/absolute/path/to/nostosx
"$NOSTOSX" add context order --dir .
"$NOSTOSX" add service order --dir .
```

A context declares domain, application, command and query packages, without fake use cases, data, databases or repositories.
A service adds app.go, private adapters and a standalone `services/order/cmd/order` entry.
The CLI reads enabled transports from the existing server constructors; it neither stores duplicate project metadata nor rewrites assembly.
[[if .GRPC]]The service includes a versioned Proto. Install Buf 1.72.0, run `task api ACTION=generate` and `go mod tidy` before building.[[else]]The service contains no Proto and needs neither Buf nor contract generation.[[end]]

For monoliths, call `order.Register([[if .HTTP]]servers.HTTP[[end]][[if and .HTTP .GRPC]], [[end]][[if .GRPC]]servers.GRPC[[end]])` in cmd/server/main.go before app.New. Compose multiple Register functions explicitly. Register only mounts handlers and has no error result; construct dependencies that can fail before mounting.
Standalone commands parse configuration and call `order.Run(ctx, cfg)`; services/order/app.go owns assembly and app.Run. Config parsing lives in infrastructure/config and transport constructors in infrastructure/server. Connect dependencies with ordinary constructors; DI is an application choice.
Keep domain/application transport-free. Introduce CQRS, narrow consumer-owned ports and stable operation metadata only for actual use cases; compose middleware in app.go when needed.

## Native HTTP operations

[[if .HTTP]]Add an HTTP adapter directly:

```sh
"$NOSTOSX" add api get-order --service order --method GET --path '/v1/orders/{order_id}'
```

The default is `--transport http`. This creates only `services/order/internal/port/http/get_order.go`: request/response types, a Handler method and an explicit registration helper. Path fields start as strings.
No Proto, OpenAPI, stub tests or business use cases are generated; no contract generation is needed.
Implement authorization, mapping and the application call, then call `RegisterGetOrder(server, handler)` from the HTTP package's Register function.
The initial handler returns a safe unimplemented error. The CLI never mounts the route or rewrites app.go automatically.
[[else]]This project only enables gRPC. HTTP listeners and adapters are absent; adding HTTP requires an explicit assembly and dependency change.[[end]]

[[if .GRPC]]## Optional Proto contracts

```sh
"$NOSTOSX" add api ping --transport grpc --service order
[[if .HTTP]]"$NOSTOSX" add api fetch-order --transport gateway --service order --method GET --path '/v1/rpc/orders/{order_id}'[[end]]
task api ACTION=generate NOSTOSX="$NOSTOSX"
[[if .HTTP]]# Only when first adding a gateway:
go get github.com/grpc-ecosystem/grpc-gateway/v2@v2.29.0[[end]]
go mod tidy
task api NOSTOSX="$NOSTOSX"
```

The grpc style creates RPCs, messages and adapters. Gateway also creates HTTP bindings and requires both transports.
Implement RPC authorization and application calls. Explicitly mount gateway handlers with HTTP authorization; an OpenAPI path does not expose a runtime route.
After editing Proto manually, `nostosx api scaffold --service order` fills missing unary Handler methods offline and preserves existing implementations. It generates no stub tests and rejects streaming scaffolds.

Proto lives in api/proto/<service>/v1; HTTP bindings and operation options live in api/http.
Pinned tools: Buf 1.72.0, protoc-gen-go v1.36.12, protoc-gen-go-grpc v1.6.0 and grpc-gateway/openapiv2 v2.29.0.
Go/gRPC/gateway output lives in api/gen/go; api/http/openapi.swagger.json uses Swagger 2. Pure gRPC does not require the gateway runtime.
Never import generated transport types into domain/application.

The Taskfile invokes CLI v1.2.0 through go run by default. NOSTOSX selects a local executable; NOSTOSX_VERSION selects a release.
`task api` lints contracts and compares temporary generated output without replacing existing files.
`task api ACTION=breaking API_BASE=<revision>` requires an explicit Git baseline; it never creates one.
Generation publishes only after all plugins succeed. Plugin failures preserve old output; ordinary publication errors attempt rollback, but process termination is not a transaction guarantee.
Contract commands execute trusted Go/Buf tools, may access the network and provide no sandbox.
[[end]]
[[if .Agent]]
## Optional agent integration

`infrastructure/agent` contains model configuration and a bounded runner. It does
not expose an HTTP/gRPC endpoint, select business tools or run automatically.
Construct one shared runner at application startup, then pass it to the use cases
that need it. A startup helper can look like this:

```go
package assembly

import (
    "errors"

    "[[.Module]]/infrastructure/agent"
)

func Agent() (*agent.Runner, error) {
    model, err := agent.ModelFromEnv()
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    if model == nil {
        return nil, errors.New("configure AGENT_PROVIDER before enabling the agent")
    }
    return agent.New(model, "Follow the application's explicit tool and authorization policy.")
}
```

Then call `runner.Ask(ctx, prompt)` from application-owned code. Pass tools to
`agent.New` explicitly; validate their inputs and authorize side effects outside
the model. Each Ask starts fresh history, accepts 1–4096 bytes of valid nonblank
UTF-8, allows at most four concurrent calls without queuing, and has a 60-second
limit, four model turns and 1024 output tokens per response. Provider failures
return a stable error without leaking backend details. Handle capacity errors at
your application boundary.

Configure one provider before running your own agent-enabled application:

```sh
export AGENT_PROVIDER=anthropic
export AGENT_MODEL='<model-name>'
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY='<private-key>'
```

For OpenAI, set `AGENT_PROVIDER=openai`, `AGENT_MODEL` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`.
`AGENT_API=responses` is the default; use `AGENT_API=chat` for Chat Completions.
Anthropic does not accept `AGENT_API`. `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` and `OPENAI_BASE_URL`
may select a trusted operator-controlled endpoint. Keys are sent there; never
accept endpoints from request input. URL credentials, queries and fragments are
rejected and redirects are disabled. No model is selected implicitly; without
`AGENT_PROVIDER`, `ModelFromEnv` returns nil. Tests use local fixtures, not these keys.
[[end]]
## Verification and maintenance

```sh
task check INTEGRATION=true
task lint
task vulncheck
[[if .GRPC]]task api[[end]]
```

The eight general tasks are `check`, `test`, `build`, `lint`, `vulncheck`, `fmt`,
`fix` and `tidy`; bare `task` runs `check`. Tests accept `RACE=true`, `COVER=true`
and `INTEGRATION=true`; coverage also enables race detection. Check enables race detection and forwards the integration
choice. CI enables integration tests; default tests need only local Go tooling.
[[if .GRPC]]`task api` defaults to non-mutating contract lint/drift checks. Select
`ACTION=generate`, `ACTION=lint` or `ACTION=breaking` for the other operations.
[[end]]

Check covers formatting, modernization, tidy dependencies, vet, builds and shuffled race tests. Listener tests use the integration tag.
Tests require no Docker, shared databases or real model credentials. `nostosx doctor --dir .` reads project/tool state and skips Buf when no Proto exists.
CLI additions and contract operations share .nostosx.lock; never remove an active owner's lock.
Template upgrades do not rewrite this project. Review source changes before applying them. See CONTRIBUTING, SECURITY, CHANGELOG and LICENSE.
